    {
      "page_title": "Iterable vs Iterator",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Two Different Things",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Iterable: any object that can produce an iterator.",
            "It implements `__iter__()` and nothing more.",
            "Iterator: the object that actually produces values.",
            "It implements both `__iter__()` and `__next__()`.",
            "Every iterator is iterable — its `__iter__` returns self."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The reverse is false. Most iterables are not iterators."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "next([1, 2, 3])          # TypeError",
            "it = iter([1, 2, 3])     # list_iterator",
            "next(it), next(it)       # 1, 2",
            "next(it)                 # 3",
            "next(it)                 # StopIteration"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Reuse vs Exhaustion",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Iterables are reusable: fresh iterator per `for`.",
            "Iterators are single-use and exhaust permanently.",
            "One list, many independent cursors over it."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Containers You Already Use"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "list, tuple, dict, set, str.",
            "range, file objects, `collections.deque`.",
            "`range(10)` is a lazy *sequence*, not a generator.",
            "It is indexable, reusable, and has `len` — genexps aren't."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Runtime Check"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "from collections.abc import Iterable",
            "from collections.abc import Iterator",
            "isinstance([1], Iterable)      # True",
            "isinstance([1], Iterator)      # False",
            "isinstance(iter([1]), Iterator) # True"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Book & Bookmark",
          "text": "Iterable = the book. Iterator = the bookmark. One book holds many bookmarks; each bookmark walks forward once."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. iter() and next() Wiring",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`iter(obj)` calls `type(obj).__iter__(obj)`.",
            "Looked up on the *type*, so `__getattr__` is bypassed.",
            "`next(it)` calls `type(it).__next__(it)`.",
            "`next(it, default)` returns default instead of raising.",
            "`StopIteration` is control flow, not an error."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. The __getitem__ Fallback"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "No `__iter__`? `iter()` builds a sequence iterator.",
            "It calls `__getitem__` with 0, 1, 2…",
            "It stops when `IndexError` is raised."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "iter(obj)",
              "next(it)",
              "body",
              "StopIteration",
              "exit"
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "The for Loop, Sentinels & Exhaustion",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "it = iter(obj)        # for x in obj:",
            "while True:",
            "    try: x = next(it)",
            "    except StopIteration: break",
            "    ...               # loop body"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "class Seq:",
            "    def __getitem__(s, i):",
            "        if i > 2: raise IndexError",
            "        return i * 10",
            "list(Seq())   # [0, 10, 20]"
          ],
          "note": "legacy fallback, still honoured"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`for` calls `iter()` exactly **once**, up front.",
            "Then `next()` on every pass until `StopIteration`.",
            "The loop swallows the exception — you never see it."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Dunders Live On The Class",
          "text": "Setting `obj.__iter__ = f` on an instance changes nothing — `iter()` only consults `type(obj)`."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. The Two-Argument iter()",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "# call until the sentinel comes back",
            "for blk in iter(lambda: f.read(4096), b''):",
            "    process(blk)      # stops at b''"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The callable takes no arguments — wrap with `lambda`.",
            "Loop ends when it returns the ==sentinel== exactly.",
            "Replaces a `while True` + `break` read loop.",
            "Ideal for sockets, queues and fixed-size reads."
          ],
          "style": "check"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Call",
            "On exhaustion"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "next(it)",
              "raises StopIteration"
            ],
            [
              "next(it, None)",
              "returns None"
            ],
            [
              "for x in it",
              "exits quietly"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "8. Once Dead, Always Dead",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "An exhausted iterator raises `StopIteration` forever.",
            "It must never resurrect — the closing invariant.",
            "PEP 234 shipped this protocol in Python 2.2.",
            "Every loop construct in the language rests on it."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "sticky",
          "title": "No Rewind",
          "text": "Iterators have no reset. To walk again, call `iter()` on the source iterable — or rebuild the generator."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "9. Everything Drives The Protocol"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Unpacking: `a, b = pair`.",
            "`list()`, `sum()`, the `in` operator.",
            "`*args` splatting and every comprehension."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "it = iter('ab')",
            "next(it)   # 'a'",
            "next(it)   # 'b'",
            "next(it)   # StopIteration"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "A Minimal Iterator Class",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "10. Minimal Iterator Class",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "class Count:",
            "    def __init__(s, n): s.i, s.n = 0, n",
            "    def __iter__(s): return s   # self"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Consumer",
            "Pulls until"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "list(it)",
              "exhausted"
            ],
            [
              "x in it",
              "first match"
            ],
            [
              "any(it)",
              "first truthy"
            ],
            [
              "a, b = it",
              "exactly 2, else ValueError"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Hand-Rolled Iterators & the yield Keyword",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "    def __next__(s):",
            "        if s.i >= s.n:",
            "            raise StopIteration",
            "        s.i += 1",
            "        return s.i - 1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`__iter__` returns self; `__next__` advances state.",
            "State lives in instance attributes.",
            "You move the cursor manually — hence the verbosity."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "11. Container vs Its Iterator"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Let the container's `__iter__` return a NEW object.",
            "That keeps the container re-iterable.",
            "Container returning `self` is the anti-pattern.",
            "A second `for` loop then sees an empty sequence."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "12. ABCs & When Classes Win"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Subclass `abc.Iterator`: `__iter__` comes free.",
            "You supply only `__next__`.",
            "`abc.Iterable` registration fixes structural typing.",
            "Classes win for extra methods, pickling, reset."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Form",
            "Lines",
            "Failure mode"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Iterator class",
              "~12",
              "no StopIteration = hang"
            ],
            [
              "Generator func",
              "~3",
              "exhausts once"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Identical Behaviour",
          "text": "Twelve lines of class and three lines of generator produce the same values. Pick the class only when you need the object, not the loop."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "13. yield Rewrites The Function",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Any `yield` in the body makes it a generator function.",
            "Calling it runs NO code at all.",
            "It returns a generator object instead.",
            "That object is already an iterator, for free."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "def gen():",
            "    print('a'); yield 1",
            "    print('b'); yield 2",
            "g = gen()    # nothing printed",
            "next(g)      # prints a, returns 1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`yield` suspends the frame and hands out a value.",
            "All locals and the instruction pointer survive.",
            "`next()` resumes right after the yield expression."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Generator Lifecycle, State & close()",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "14. How A Generator Ends",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Falling off the end raises `StopIteration` for you.",
            "`return value` sets `StopIteration.value`.",
            "Read it via `yield from` or by catching the exception."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "15. Legal & Common Shapes"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Many yields in one body.",
            "Yields inside `while` and `for` loops.",
            "Yields inside `try/finally` blocks."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Resumable Function",
          "text": "A generator is cooperative multitasking in miniature, not a data structure. `return` destroys the frame; `yield` freezes it on the heap for later."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "16. Generator Object Attributes",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Attribute",
            "Holds"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "gi_frame",
              "suspended frame or None"
            ],
            [
              "gi_code",
              "the code object"
            ],
            [
              "gi_running",
              "executing flag"
            ],
            [
              "gi_yieldfrom",
              "delegated subgenerator"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "17. Four Lifecycle States"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "CREATED",
              "RUNNING",
              "SUSPENDED",
              "CLOSED"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "from inspect import getgeneratorstate",
            "getgeneratorstate(g)  # GEN_SUSPENDED",
            "g.gi_frame.f_lasti    # resume offset"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The frame is heap-allocated, kept alive by the generator.",
            "That is why locals survive suspension.",
            "`CO_GENERATOR` on the code object triggers all this.",
            "`gi_frame.f_locals` shows a paused generator's variables."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "18. close() and GeneratorExit",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`g.close()` throws `GeneratorExit` at the pause point.",
            "The generator must not yield again after it.",
            "`try/finally` and `with` run cleanup on close or GC."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Misuse",
            "Error raised"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "yield after GeneratorExit",
              "RuntimeError: ignored GeneratorExit"
            ],
            [
              "next() while running",
              "ValueError: already executing"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Reference cycles round a paused frame delay cleanup.",
            "Never trust GC timing to release a file handle."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Lazy Evaluation & Streaming Pipelines",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "19. Laziness Defined",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A value is produced only when it is requested.",
            "Work is deferred, and possibly never done at all.",
            "One frame plus one item: O(1) space, any length."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "20. The Memory Number"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Form",
            "10M squares"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "[x*x for x in ...]",
              "~400 MB"
            ],
            [
              "(x*x for x in ...)",
              "a few hundred bytes"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "21. Short-Circuit & Infinite Streams",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "hit = next(x for x in huge if pred(x))",
            "def naturals():",
            "    n = 0",
            "    while True: yield n; n += 1",
            "islice(naturals(), 10)   # bounded"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The search stops at the first match, not the last item.",
            "Infinite streams become expressible and safe.",
            "Bound them with `islice` or a `break` in the consumer."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "22. Streaming Pipelines"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "lines = (l.strip() for l in f)",
            "nums = (int(l) for l in lines if l.isdigit())",
            "total = sum(nums)   # one pass, O(1) mem"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Iterating a file object yields one line at a time.",
            "Never `f.readlines()` on a multi-GB log.",
            "Each stage pulls exactly one item from the stage below."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "The Price",
          "text": "Time-to-first-result drops to near zero, but total throughput dips from per-item overhead — and side-effect timing gets much harder to reason about."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "23. Genexp Syntax",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "(x * x for x in data)        # genexp",
            "sum(x * x for x in data)     # parens ok",
            "func((x for x in data), key) # parens req"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Parentheses instead of the list comp's brackets.",
            "Optional when the genexp is the sole argument.",
            "Two arguments need explicit parens, else `SyntaxError`."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The outermost iterable is passed in like an argument.",
            "So its errors surface at ==creation==, not first `next()`.",
            "`(x for x in 5)` raises `TypeError` immediately."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Late Binding & the Eager Outermost Iterable",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
